		{
			"id": "at://did:plc:rzf7l6olyl67yfy2jwufdq7f/app.bsky.feed.post/3lsj74praac2u",
			"parentId": "at://did:plc:bsax2z6bbugsvljtmzwq4vrv/app.bsky.feed.post/3lsisbgp2nc27",
			"url": "https://bsky.app/profile/humanwhocodes.com/post/3lsj74praac2u",
			"author": {
				"name": "Nicholas C. Zakas",
				"url": "https://bsky.app/profile/humanwhocodes.com",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/avatar/plain/did:plc:rzf7l6olyl67yfy2jwufdq7f/bafkreibbsrcnu4eml6civfhrgbmfanj3xy7gztrjsp2klrhdgfe3obp4he"
			},
			"date": "2025-06-26T13:09:26.092Z",
			"content": "<p>The premise of “backwards compatibility” is that the other thing is old and your thing is new. You can’t be backwards compatible with a competitor. You can be compatible, though.</p>\n<p>FWIW I’m glad Node.js compat is finally here. Just maybe the marketing folks don’t grok the relationship.</p>",
			"via": "bluesky"
		},
